From 1b007d8d75d4659fd977a069be87ef28c5a33323 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: martinsngr <martin.sinegre@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 14:24:58 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] G: opt-in porous/free faces as two-phase Darcy faces
 (porousFreeDarcyFaces)

Problem. p is the mixture pressure p_air - alpha*pc in porous cells and the
fluid pressure in free cells. On porous/free faces (Solidf = 0) the solver
used the momentum flux with no capillary term, i.e. p continuous: next to a
free cell holding air, the air pressure in the porous cell exceeded the free
air pressure by alpha*pc (about 4-5 kPa for Van Genuchten pc0 5000 Pa, m 0.5,
even when dry). A wet porous region next to free fluid then drains instead
of wicking, and in spontaneous imbibition the error is of the order of the
capillary drive itself. The physical condition at a porous/free face is
continuity of the air pressure (free cell holding air) or of the water
pressure (free cell holding water) (cf. Mosthaf et al. 2011, WRR).

Change (keyword porousFreeDarcyFaces in transportProperties, default off):
- porousFace.H (new): porousFace = 1 on faces with at least one porous side
  (= Solidf without the keyword). It replaces Solidf in the phase Darcy
  fluxes and the alpha flux split (alphaEqn.H), the kr upwinding
  (updateVariables.H) and the Darcy-face selection (createPorousMediaFields).
- updateVariables.H: pc = dpc/dS = 0 in free cells. With linear face
  interpolation the phase fluxes on porous/free faces are then driven by the
  differences of the phase pressures p_air = p + alpha*pc and
  p_water = p - (1 - alpha)*pc, continuous across the face.
- createPorousMediaFields.H: harmonic permeability on porous/free faces (the
  porous half cell carries the resistance; linear interpolation gave
  (K + K_free)/2); start-up mobilities and pc recomputed once if such faces
  exist (they were built before Solid was known); count of porous/free faces
  and a warning if some are not Darcy faces (darcyFaceMaxDa too small for
  them), where the coupling would not apply.
- alphaEqn.H: on porous/free faces the alpha flux is the water Darcy flux
  phiWetting itself. The drift form (alpha_up*phi + relative flux), kept
  between porous cells, gives the wrong phase split across the alpha and eps
  jumps of a porous/free face (it pushed water out of the porous cell at
  about 1.3 times an incoming air flux).

Checks (2D bench: porous slab between free layers, 20 um cells, K 1e-11,
VG pc0 5000 Pa m 0.5; free cells initialised as pure air): closed ramp
without reservoir: water stays in the porous slab (+0.0000 % over 5 ms;
without G -42 % in 0.2 ms) and its saturation profile matches the same slab
without free cells to 4e-5; static wet slab: at rest, p in the slab =
-alpha*pc next to free air at 0; dry slab next to a water-filled free layer
imbibes (without G: almost nothing); wicking bench with air-filled free
layers runs to 5 ms (without G: collapse at 18 us) and follows the no-free-
cell reference with a small speed-up (lateral air escape). Processor and
cyclic boundaries on the porous/free faces: same results as internal faces
within solver tolerance. Without porous/free faces, or without the keyword:
bit-identical to the previous solver (1D wicking column to 0.1 s, all-porous
bench).

Open: free cells must start as pure air (alpha 0), not at the porous dry
value, otherwise surface-tension currents grow on small alpha variations;
air jets of a few m/s remain near a free inlet next to open boundaries
(numerical, not explained); exact phase-pressure differences only for 0.5
interpolation weights; momentumPredictor path not adapted.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
